<!-- SECTION 15A: LEGAL FRAMEWORK -->
<section class="content-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <h2 class="section-title">Legal &amp; Compliance Framework for F&amp;O Taxation</h2>
            <div class="content-text">
                
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Section 43(5):</strong> F&amp;O classified as non-speculative business income &ndash; transactions on recognised exchanges excluded from speculative definition</li>
                    <li><strong>Section 44AB:</strong> Tax audit if turnover &gt; Rs 1 crore (Rs 10 crore if 95%+ digital); also if profit &lt; 6% with 44AD opt-out</li>
                    <li><strong>Section 44AD:</strong> Presumptive taxation &ndash; 6% of turnover (digital), turnover up to Rs 3 crore. No books/audit required</li>
                    <li><strong>F&amp;O Turnover:</strong> Absolute sum of trade-wise P&amp;L + option premium received + reverse trade differences</li>
                    <li><strong>Tax Audit Report:</strong> Form 3CB-3CD for non-company traders, uploaded by 30 September</li>
                    <li><strong>Loss Set-Off:</strong> Non-speculative business loss can offset any income except salary in current year</li>
                    <li><strong>Loss Carry-Forward:</strong> 8 years &ndash; against non-speculative business income only. Filing by due date mandatory for carry-forward</li>
                    <li><strong>STT:</strong> 0.02% on futures sale, 0.1% on options sale (Budget 2024 increase)</li>
                    <li><strong>Books of Accounts:</strong> Mandatory if income &gt; Rs 2.5 lakh or gross receipts &gt; Rs 25 lakh in preceding 3 years</li>
                    <li><strong>Section 271B:</strong> Penalty for non-audit &ndash; 0.5% of turnover (max Rs 1,50,000)</li>
                </ul>
                <p><strong>Filing Portal:</strong> <a href="https://www.incometax.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">incometax.gov.in</a></p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ SECTION -->
<section id="faq-section" class="content-section" style="background-color: #ffffff;">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <div class="faq-expanded">
                <aside class="faq-expanded__aside">
                    <h2 class="faq-expanded__title">Frequently Asked Questions &ndash; ITR for F&amp;O Traders in Mumbai</h2>
                    <p class="faq-expanded__lead">Get answers about F&amp;O turnover computation, tax audit, ITR forms, loss set-off, presumptive taxation, and more for Mumbai traders.</p>
                    @include('partials.bigin-form', [
                        'variant'  => 'compact',
                        'service'  => 'ITR for F&amp;O Traders',
                        'city'     => 'Mumbai',
                    ])
                </aside>
                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Which IT office handles F&amp;O ITR in Mumbai?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Filed electronically on incometax.gov.in and processed by CPC Bengaluru. Annual Information Statement captures all F&amp;O transactions from exchanges. Jurisdictional AO (based on PAN zone) handles scrutiny. No physical filing required. Patron handles all electronic filings for Mumbai F&amp;O clients.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Which ITR form should F&amp;O traders use?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>ITR-3 for regular F&amp;O business income with P&amp;L and balance sheet. ITR-4 if opting for presumptive taxation under Section 44AD (declare 6% profit). ITR-1 and ITR-2 cannot be used for F&amp;O income. For salaried traders with F&amp;O, salary and F&amp;O are reported together in ITR-3.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">How is F&amp;O turnover calculated?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Absolute sum of profit or loss on each individual squared-off trade plus premium received on options sold. NOT total buy/sell trade value. For futures: absolute value of (sell &minus; buy) &times; quantity. For options: absolute value of (sell premium &minus; buy premium) &times; quantity. A Mumbai trader with Rs 1 crore trade value may have turnover of only Rs 3-5 lakh.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">When is tax audit required for F&amp;O traders?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Turnover &gt; Rs 10 crore: mandatory. Rs 1-10 crore with cash &gt; 5%: mandatory. Rs 1-10 crore with &le;5% cash (most digital traders): no audit. Under Rs 1 crore: no audit typically needed. Opted out of 44AD with profit &lt; 6% and income above exemption: audit required. Most retail Mumbai traders need no audit.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can F&amp;O losses be set off against salary income?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>No. F&amp;O losses (non-speculative business) cannot offset salary income. They can offset house property, interest, capital gains, and other business income in the current year. Carry forward 8 years against non-speculative business income only. This restriction particularly impacts Mumbai's BKC and Nariman Point salaried professionals trading F&amp;O part-time.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Is F&amp;O income speculative?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>No. F&amp;O is explicitly non-speculative under Section 43(5). Derivatives on recognised exchanges (BSE, NSE, MCX) are excluded from speculative definition. This is beneficial because non-speculative losses have broader set-off rights (against any income except salary) compared to speculative losses (only against speculative income).</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can I use Section 44AD (presumptive) for F&amp;O trading?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes, if turnover is within Rs 3 crore (95%+ digital). Declare 6% of turnover as profit (digital) or 8% (cash). No books or audit needed. However, if you have actual losses, declaring 6% profit means paying tax on non-existent income. Opting for 44AD locks you in for 5 years. For Mumbai traders with frequent losses, regular ITR-3 is usually more beneficial.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>


            <!-- Quick Answers -->
            <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:32px;">
                <p><strong>Quick Answers</strong></p>
                <p><strong>F&amp;O trading pe tax kaise lagta hai?</strong> F&amp;O income non-speculative business income hai. Slab rates se tax lagta hai (capital gains rate nahi). ITR-3 mein PGBP head mein file karo. Brokerage, internet, advisory subscription sab deduct ho sakta hai.</p>
                <p><strong>F&amp;O ka turnover kaise calculate kare?</strong> Har trade ka profit ya loss ka absolute value jodo. Option premium received bhi add karo. Total buy-sell value nahi hai ye. Rs 50 lakh trade value pe turnover sirf Rs 2-5 lakh ho sakta hai.</p>
                <p><strong>F&amp;O loss salary se adjust ho sakta hai kya?</strong> Nahi. F&amp;O loss salary income se set off nahi hota. House property income, interest, capital gains, ya doosre business income se adjust ho sakta hai. 8 saal tak carry forward bhi ho sakta hai.</p>

            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

